---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ReviewedByCard from "../../components/ReviewedByCard.astro";
import { TRUST_PROFILES } from "../../config/trust";

const title = "Accelerated Payment Plans (How they work)";
const description =
  "How to evaluate a third-party acceleration plan versus making the same annual extra payment on your own. Educational use only.";

const lastUpdated = "2026-04-22";
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/guides/extra-payment-accelerated-plan"
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  authorProfile={TRUST_PROFILES.siteOwner}
  reviewProfiles={[TRUST_PROFILES.editorialReview, TRUST_PROFILES.methodologyReview]}
>
  <section class="card hero">
    <span class="pill">Guide</span>
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/guides">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Accelerated plan</span>
    </nav>
    <h1>Accelerated payment plans</h1>
    <p>
      This page is for one narrow question: should you pay for a third-party acceleration plan, or can you get nearly the same result by
      sending the same annual dollars on your own? The real test is not the marketing language. It is fees, posting rules, and whether
      the program solves a behavior problem you have not solved manually.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/calculators/biweekly-mortgage-payment-calculator">Biweekly calculator</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/calculators/extra-payment-calculator">Extra payment calculator</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/biweekly-vs-extra-principal">Biweekly vs extra principal</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ReviewedByCard
    writtenBy={TRUST_PROFILES.siteOwner}
    reviewedBy={TRUST_PROFILES.editorialReview}
    secondaryReview={TRUST_PROFILES.methodologyReview}
    reviewScope="Acceleration-plan framing, fee comparison logic, and routing between biweekly, DIY extra-payment, and lender-posting workflows."
    reviewedOn={lastUpdated}
  />

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Use this page when the sales pitch sounds smarter than the math</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Use this page when a third-party acceleration plan is being sold as a payment hack rather than a fee-bearing service.</li>
      <li>Use this page when you need to compare same annual dollars on your own against the enrolled version.</li>
      <li>Leave this page when the real question is simply biweekly versus monthly extra. That belongs to <a href="/guides/biweekly-vs-extra-principal">biweekly vs extra principal</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:12px 0 0">
      If the real question is "biweekly or monthly extra?", go next to biweekly vs extra principal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">What this page is really comparing</h2>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">
      A third-party acceleration plan is often framed as a smarter payment rhythm. The fair benchmark is simpler: if you send the same
      annual dollars on your own, do you keep most of the savings without the middleman and its fee drag?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">The fair comparison: same annual dollars on your own</h2>
    <ol class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Calculate how much the program collects from you over 12 months.</li>
      <li>Subtract setup fees, draft fees, and ongoing servicing fees.</li>
      <li>Model the same annual dollars on your own in the <a href="/calculators/extra-payment-calculator">extra payment calculator</a>.</li>
      <li>Only then decide whether the program is solving a real problem or just repackaging discipline.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="grid" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="card section prose col-6">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">When a plan may help</h2>
      <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
        <li>The friction is behavioral, not mathematical, and automation keeps you consistent.</li>
        <li>Your servicer posts principal quickly and the fee drag is genuinely small.</li>
        <li>The plan solves a problem you have repeatedly failed to solve manually.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card section prose col-6">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">When DIY usually wins</h2>
      <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
        <li>You can already automate transfers yourself.</li>
        <li>The sales story is mostly about "more frequent payments" without proving faster principal posting.</li>
        <li>Fee drag takes back enough savings that the plan underperforms a simple DIY transfer.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where fee drag shows up</h2>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">
      Fee drag often hides inside enrollment charges, monthly service fees, or bank-draft fees. The smaller your extra payment, the more
      those charges matter. A plan collecting one extra payment per year can still lose against a DIY version if enough cash leaks out
      before it reaches principal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Questions worth asking before you enroll</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Is the plan run by my actual servicer or by a third party that forwards payments later?</li>
      <li>When does the principal actually post after my bank account is drafted?</li>
      <li>Can I pause, lower, or cancel the plan without penalties?</li>
      <li>What total fees will I pay in year one?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">A practical routing rule</h2>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">
      If the real question is "biweekly or monthly extra?", go next to <a href="/guides/biweekly-vs-extra-principal">biweekly vs extra principal</a>.
      If the real question is "can I reproduce this myself?", stay with the <a href="/calculators/extra-payment-calculator">extra payment calculator</a>.
      If the real question is "will my servicer apply this correctly?", move next to <a href="/guides/extra-payment-servicer-posting-rules">servicer posting rules</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">References</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>
        CFPB:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Owning a home
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:10px">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/calculators/extra-payment-calculator">Model the DIY version</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/biweekly-vs-extra-principal">Compare biweekly vs monthly extra</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/extra-payment-servicer-posting-rules">Check posting rules</a>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:12px 0 0">Educational use only. Not financial advice.</p>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:12px 0 0; font-size:13px">Last updated: 2026-04-22</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
